# {{ company_name }} customer-service playbook

## What to write here

The company-wide operating instructions that decide *what to do, and what to
write*, for every kind of request {{ company_name }} support receives. This is
the one file every matching procedure is applied from before a reply is
composed — across every mailbox of this company, not only this clone's own.

- **Per kind of request, a short workflow.** Name the request (a refund, a
  cancellation, a shipping question, a technical fault, a pricing question,
  a partnership pitch, …), then say what to check, what to write, and what
  never to promise. Group related requests instead of writing one entry per
  possible phrasing.
- **Product and service facts** a reply may state as true: what {{ company_name }}
  sells, how it works, what it does not do, and the limits a customer needs to
  hear before they act on an answer.
- **Escalation.** Which requests this operator must never answer itself —
  anything contractual, anything about money it cannot verify, anything where
  a wrong answer costs more than a slow one — and who or what the escalation
  hands to.
- **What this company never says.** Claims, promises or phrasings that must
  never appear in a reply, even when a customer asks for them directly.

Write it as instructions to the operator, not as a story about how the company
came to work this way — say what holds today, not how it got there.

## Why it matters

This is the one place a stated rule about *what to do or write for a kind of
request* belongs, company-wide. An agent that reads this file before drafting
applies every matching workflow; an agent that cannot find the rule here has
no way to know it exists. A rule stated once in a chat session and never
written here is lost the moment the session ends.

This file compiles into the standing instructions every profile of this
company receives (`cs instructions`) and into the phone assistant's product
notes — so a workflow written here reaches every channel, not only mail.
